<!-- ── §01 ── -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Left-to-Right Bottleneck</div>
<h2>Why AR-style OPSD Fails for dLLMs</h2>
<p>On-policy Self-Distillation (OPSD) lets a model teach itself: the same network acts as both student (generating responses) and teacher (supervising the student). For autoregressive (AR) models, the self-teacher is constructed by appending a reference solution to the prompt — the teacher sees "question + reference answer → what token comes next?" while the student only sees the question.</p>
<p>This works for left-to-right models because they are <em>prefix-conditioned</em>: every prediction looks only leftward. Appending text to the left of the answer makes it richer context. But for diffusion LLMs (dLLMs), this design has a critical flaw:</p>

<div class="callout callout-amber">
  <strong>The AR OPSD failure mode:</strong> when you append a reference solution as prefix context, the teacher's token-level distribution becomes nearly <em>identical</em> to the student's — there is almost no new knowledge to transfer. The student learns nothing useful.
</div>

<p>The root cause is that AR OPSD borrows privileged information from an external dataset ("here is the ground-truth solution, conditioned on it"). This static reference is already so similar to what the model predicts that the teacher-student overlap approaches 98%. d-OPSD addresses this with a fundamentally different insight: dLLMs can look both ways.</p>

<p>Diffusion LLMs are bidirectional: they can model not just <em>p(suffix | prefix)</em> like AR models, but also <em>p(prefix | suffix)</em> — given the end of a sequence, fill in the middle. This opens a new door: instead of peeking at someone else's solution, the teacher peeks at the <strong>student's own generated answer</strong>. The student sees a blurry future; the teacher sees a sharper one.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">AR-style vs d-OPSD teacher construction</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">prefix vs suffix conditioning</span>
  </div>
