        <button id="dn-reset" style="padding:.3rem .8rem;border-radius:5px;font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);cursor:pointer;border:1px solid var(--border-hi);background:var(--surface);color:var(--muted2)">↺ Reset</button>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div style="font-size:.68rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted);margin-bottom:.5rem">Step: <span id="dn-step">T (all masked)</span></div>
    <div class="denoise-grid" id="dn-grid"></div>
    <div style="margin-top:.75rem;display:flex;gap:1rem;flex-wrap:wrap">
      <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.4rem;font-size:.68rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">
        <span style="width:14px;height:14px;border-radius:3px;background:rgba(255,255,255,.04);border:1px solid var(--border);display:inline-block"></span> MASKED
      </div>
      <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.4rem;font-size:.68rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">
        <span style="width:14px;height:14px;border-radius:3px;background:rgba(16,185,129,.12);border:1px solid rgba(16,185,129,.4);display:inline-block"></span> REVEALED
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The key observation: at each step, the model doesn't just see the question and some already-revealed tokens — it sees the <em>entire context</em> bidirectionally. A token at position 5 in the answer can attend to hints from position 15 even before position 15 is fully revealed. This bidirectional attention is what enables suffix conditioning.</p>
</section>

<!-- ── §03 ── -->
<section id="s-teacher" data-code="teacher">
<div class="section-no">03 — The Self-Future Teacher</div>
<h2>Peeking at Your Own Generated Answer</h2>
<p>Here's d-OPSD's core idea. Once the student model generates a complete trajectory (question → fully denoised answer), we go back to each denoising step t and ask: <em>what if the model had peeked at a fraction ρ of the tokens in its own final answer?</em></p>
<p>That "peeking model" — the same neural network, but conditioned on partial knowledge of the future it will generate — becomes the <strong>self-teacher</strong>. The student (no peeking) and teacher (partial peeking) use identical weights, differing only in their input context. The teacher's extra knowledge comes entirely from tokens the student itself produced.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Teacher input at denoising step t</div>
  <span class="math-var">yteacher,t</span>[i] = <span class="math-var">y₀</span>[i] if i ∈ Sₜ,  else <span class="math-var">yₜ</span>[i]
  <span class="math-comment"> ← Sₜ = randomly sampled ρ-fraction of still-masked positions</span><br>
  <span class="math-var">yₜ</span> = student input (no peeking)
  <span class="math-comment"> ← y₀ = final generated answer (the "self-future")</span>
</div>

<p>The <strong>retaining ratio ρ</strong> controls how much of the future is revealed. Use the buttons below to see what each ρ means concretely — and how teacher accuracy (from the toy verification experiment) grows with ρ.</p>

<div class="vp">
