</section>

<!-- ── §04 ── -->
<section id="s-divergence" data-code="divergence">
<div class="section-no">04 — Step-Level KL Alignment</div>
<h2>Supervising the Denoising Process, Not Individual Tokens</h2>
<p>The final piece of d-OPSD is <em>how</em> the teacher guides the student. AR models use token-level KL divergence: for each position i in the sequence, minimize KL(student dist || teacher dist). But dLLMs don't predict tokens one by one — they predict all masked tokens simultaneously at each step, then keep only the top-k.</p>
<p>Token-level supervision is incompatible with this process. Instead, d-OPSD uses <strong>step-level divergence</strong>: at each denoising step t, compute KL only over the top-k most confident token positions — exactly the positions that determine the step-level transition from yₜ to yₜ₋₁.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">d-OPSD training objective</div>
  <span class="math-var">L</span>(θ) = 𝔼ₓ [ (1/T) Σₜ (1/|Kₜ|) Σᵢ∈Kₜ DKL( <span class="math-var">pθ(y|ystudent,t,x)</span> || <span class="math-var">pθ(y|yteacher,t,x)</span> ) ]<br>
  where <span class="math-var">Kₜ</span> = top-k most confident masked positions at step t (from teacher distribution)<br>
  ΣₜT |Kₜ| = L
  <span class="math-comment"> ← every token position covered exactly once across T steps</span>
</div>

<p>Walk through the algorithm step by step:</p>

<div class="step-list" id="step-list">
  <div class="step-box active" data-step="0">
    <div class="step-num">1</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Student samples on-policy trajectory</div>
      <div class="step-desc">For prompt x, student pθ generates a complete T-step denoising trajectory Y = &#123;yT, ..., y0&#125;. We keep sampling until a correct answer appears (up to k=8 tries).</div>
      <div class="step-code">Y ~ pθ(·|x);  // y_T = all-masked, y_0 = final answer</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" data-step="1">
    <div class="step-num">2</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Construct teacher input at each step t</div>
      <div class="step-desc">For each denoising step t, randomly reveal ρ fraction of the masked positions using y₀ (the student's own final answer). Student keeps its original noisy input yₜ.</div>
      <div class="step-code">yteacher,t[i] = y0[i] if i ∈ Sₜ else yₜ[i]</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" data-step="2">
    <div class="step-num">3</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Identify top-k confident positions Kₜ</div>
      <div class="step-desc">From the teacher's distribution, find the k masked positions with highest confidence. These are the tokens the model "commits to" at this step.</div>
      <div class="step-code">Kₜ = top-k indices from teacher dist (masked positions only)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" data-step="3">
    <div class="step-num">4</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Compute step-level KL over Kₜ</div>
      <div class="step-desc">For only the top-k positions in Kₜ, compute KL divergence between student's and teacher's token distributions. Average over steps.</div>
      <div class="step-code">Lₜ = (1/|Kₜ|) Σᵢ∈Kₜ DKL(P_student,t^i || P_teacher,t^i)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" data-step="4">
    <div class="step-num">5</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Backpropagate and update</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Gradient flows through the step-level KL objective to update θ. Per-token pointwise KL clipping (threshold 0.05) stabilizes training and prevents collapse.</div>
      <div class="step-code">θ ← θ - ∇ [(1/T) Σₜ Lₜ]   // clipped KL</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="step-nav">
  <button id="step-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
  <span id="step-prog" style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">Step 1 of 5</span>
  <button id="step-next">Next →</button>
</div>

<p>One engineering trick makes this tractable: instead of running T forward passes through the model (one per denoising step), all step inputs are <em>concatenated</em> into a single batch of shape (bsz×T, seq-length). Because all steps share identical weights, gradients are constant per input, eliminating the memory explosion from storing T separate attention maps.</p>
</section>

<!-- ── §05 ── -->
<section id="s-overlap" data-code="overlap">
<div class="section-no">05 — The Overlap Problem</div>
<h2>Why Self-Future Beats Reference Solutions</h2>
<p>Why does d-OPSD's teacher construction outperform AR-style OPSD? The paper introduces a metric called <strong>Overlap Top-K</strong>: at each denoising step, it measures the fraction of vocabulary tokens that appear in both the student's and teacher's top-K distributions at the currently-unmasked positions.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Overlap Top-K metric</div>
  <span class="math-var">M_overlap</span>(K,t) = (1/|Kₜ|) Σᵢ∈Kₜ |P_student,t^&#123;i,Top-K&#125; ∩ P_teacher,t^&#123;i,Top-K&#125;| / K
  <span class="math-comment"> ← K=20 in practice</span>
</div>

<p>If overlap = 1.0, teacher and student distributions are identical — the student learns nothing. If overlap = 0.5, about half the top-20 vocabulary differs — meaningful knowledge transfer. The experiment below shows what happens with AR-style OPSD vs d-OPSD:</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Overlap Top-K: how much new knowledge does the teacher provide?</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">same token position, different conditioning</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="overlap-row">
      <div class="overlap-card">
        <div class="overlap-title">AR-style OPSD — appended reference solution</div>
        <div id="overlap-ar"></div>
        <div class="overlap-stat bad">Overlap ≈ 0.98 — near-identical distributions<br><small>Teacher adds almost no new knowledge to transfer</small></div>
      </div>
      <div class="overlap-card">
        <div class="overlap-title">d-OPSD — self-generated suffix conditioning</div>
        <div id="overlap-dopsd"></div>
        <div class="overlap-stat good">Overlap ≈ 0.65 — meaningfully different distributions<br><small>~35% of teacher's top-20 vocab is new to the student</small></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="callout" style="margin-top:.5rem">
      <strong>Intuition:</strong> Appending a <em>reference solution</em> (AR-style) provides text the model has likely trained on — its predictions barely change. Revealing the model's <em>own generated answer</em> as suffix context forces genuinely different reasoning: the model fills in missing reasoning steps knowing where it needs to arrive.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>This explains the performance gap: AR-style OPSD gives +2.4pp on GSM8K (76.0 → 78.4%), but d-OPSD gives +5.0pp (76.0 → 81.0%). The mechanism is the same as human learning — you gain more by knowing the outcome of your own decisions than by studying someone else's worked example.</p>
</section>

<!-- ── §06 ── -->
<section id="s-results" data-code="results">
<div class="section-no">06 — Results: 18× Faster Convergence</div>
<h2>Superior Performance, a Fraction of the Cost</h2>
<p>d-OPSD was evaluated on four reasoning tasks using <strong>LLaDA-8B-Instruct</strong> as the base dLLM: GSM8K and MATH500 (mathematical reasoning), plus Countdown and 4×4 Sudoku (planning). The primary baselines are <strong>diffu-GRPO</strong> (RLVR for dLLMs) and an SFT variant.</p>

<h3>Sample efficiency — steps to converge</h3>
