      <td><strong>d-OPSD</strong></td>
      <td>OPSD</td>
      <td class="mn best">82.2</td>
      <td class="mn">37.8</td>
      <td class="mn best">32.3</td>
      <td class="mn best">20.6</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>d-OPSD matches or exceeds diffu-GRPO on every task while converging dramatically faster. The gain on planning tasks (Countdown, Sudoku) is notable — planning requires constraint satisfaction rather than arithmetic, suggesting that the self-future construction generalizes beyond math reasoning.</p>

<div class="callout callout-amber">
  <strong>Known failure mode — policy collapse:</strong> Like RLVR, d-OPSD can collapse after reaching peak performance in some configurations. The reverse-KL's mode-seeking behavior becomes over-narrow, preventing further improvement. Pointwise KL clipping (threshold 0.05) substantially mitigates this but doesn't fully eliminate it.
</div>
</section>

<!-- ── Listen-friendly summary ── -->
<div class="listen-friendly-content" style="display:none;" aria-hidden="true">
  <div lang="en">
    <h2>Audio Summary: d-OPSD — Learning from Your Self-Future</h2>
    <p>d-OPSD is the first on-policy self-distillation framework designed specifically for diffusion language models. Unlike autoregressive models that generate text left-to-right, diffusion LLMs iteratively unmask tokens from a fully-masked sequence over multiple denoising steps. This bidirectional process allows them to condition on both past and future context.</p>
    <p>The key insight is teacher construction: d-OPSD uses the student model's own generated answer as privileged "future" information for the teacher. By revealing a fraction of the final answer tokens to the teacher (controlled by the retaining ratio rho), the teacher gains meaningful knowledge that the student can learn from. This contrasts with AR-style OPSD, which appends an external reference solution and achieves 98% overlap with student distributions — almost no new knowledge. d-OPSD achieves around 65% overlap, enabling substantial knowledge transfer.</p>
    <p>Instead of token-level KL divergence used in AR models, d-OPSD applies step-level KL over only the top-k most confident denoising positions at each step. Results show d-OPSD converges in as few as 100 gradient steps on MATH500 versus 6,600 for RLVR — a 66-times speedup — while matching RLVR performance on reasoning and planning benchmarks.</p>
  </div>
  <div lang="zh">
    <h2>语音摘要：d-OPSD——从自我未来中学习</h2>
    <p>d-OPSD是首个专为扩散大语言模型设计的在线自蒸馏框架。与自回归模型逐token生成不同，扩散LLM通过多步去噪过程逐步揭示被掩码的token，具备双向注意力能力，可以同时条件化过去和未来的上下文。</p>
    <p>核心创新在于教师构建方式：d-OPSD将学生模型自己生成的答案作为教师的"未来"特权信息。通过将最终答案中一定比例（保留率ρ）的token透露给教师，教师获得了学生可以学习的有意义知识。这与AR风格的OPSD形成对比——后者追加外部参考答案导致与学生分布的重叠率高达98%，几乎没有新知识可以传递；而d-OPSD的重叠率约为65%，实现了实质性的知识迁移。</p>
    <p>d-OPSD使用步骤级KL散度代替AR模型中的token级KL，仅在每步去噪中置信度最高的top-k位置上计算散度。实验结果表明，d-OPSD在MATH500上仅需100个梯度更新步骤即可收敛，而RLVR需要6600步，速度提升66倍，同时在推理和规划任务上与RLVR性能持平或更优。</p>
  </div>
</div>

